WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.444
Welcome to this Java tutorial, where you'll

00:00:03.444 --> 00:00:06.326
learn to arrange interface content in rows and

00:00:06.326 --> 00:00:09.020
columns, separate sections with spacing and

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:11.840
padding, and keep text readable when a window

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:12.654
changes size.

00:00:13.299 --> 00:00:15.779
These skills help people find related

00:00:15.779 --> 00:00:18.327
information quickly and keep important

00:00:18.327 --> 00:00:21.478
instructions visible as an application’s window

00:00:21.478 --> 00:00:22.349
changes size.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.706
A campus service desk needs a notice with a

00:00:25.706 --> 00:00:28.034
heading, a room location, and a short

00:00:28.034 --> 00:00:28.789
instruction.

00:00:28.789 --> 00:00:31.306
We'll first build a reading desk notice.

00:00:31.306 --> 00:00:34.264
Then we'll adapt the same structure for a study

00:00:34.264 --> 00:00:37.222
room, keeping the location and instruction easy

00:00:37.222 --> 00:00:37.725
to find.

00:00:38.382 --> 00:00:41.037
We'll inspect the actual notice, narrow its

00:00:41.037 --> 00:00:43.631
native window, and check that the complete

00:00:43.631 --> 00:00:45.360
instruction remains visible.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.953
The console will report the room value and

00:00:47.953 --> 00:00:49.003
wrapping setting.

00:00:49.003 --> 00:00:51.720
That report supports our check, but only the

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:54.498
visible interface can show whether its layout

00:00:54.498 --> 00:00:54.807
fits.

00:00:55.475 --> 00:00:58.565
In the Workspace, we'll open Main.java and use

00:00:58.565 --> 00:01:01.656
the supplied standalone JavaFX runner from the

00:01:01.656 --> 00:01:02.731
previous lesson.

00:01:02.731 --> 00:01:05.619
Its start method receives a Stage after the

00:01:05.619 --> 00:01:06.761
toolkit is ready.

00:01:06.761 --> 00:01:09.583
Our layout instructions belong inside that

00:01:09.583 --> 00:01:10.725
interface method.

00:01:39.987 --> 00:01:42.846
The imports make the class names available.

00:01:42.846 --> 00:01:45.970
Main extends Application, and start runs on the

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:47.698
JavaFX Application Thread.

00:01:47.698 --> 00:01:50.889
The stage variable refers to the primary window.

00:01:50.889 --> 00:01:54.080
We can now create the nodes that will become its

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:54.612
content.

00:01:54.633 --> 00:01:56.962
Start with a Label for Reading Desk.

00:01:56.962 --> 00:01:59.549
A heading should stand out from ordinary

00:01:59.549 --> 00:02:02.654
instructions, so we'll give this label an inline

00:02:02.654 --> 00:02:05.564
style: presentation settings stored in a Java

00:02:05.564 --> 00:02:06.858
String on this node.

00:02:17.912 --> 00:02:20.704
Inside that String, each CSS declaration has a

00:02:20.704 --> 00:02:23.011
property name, a colon, a value, and a

00:02:23.011 --> 00:02:23.618
semicolon.

00:02:23.618 --> 00:02:26.532
The font size is twenty-two pixels, and the font

00:02:26.532 --> 00:02:27.442
weight is bold.

00:02:27.442 --> 00:02:29.688
The quotes belong to the Java String.

00:02:29.688 --> 00:02:32.541
These settings change the heading's appearance;

00:02:32.541 --> 00:02:35.273
they do not change its text or move it into a

00:02:35.273 --> 00:02:36.487
different container.

00:02:36.529 --> 00:02:39.486
Next, create a label for Room and a separate

00:02:39.486 --> 00:02:40.696
label for Library.

00:02:40.696 --> 00:02:43.653
Those two pieces form one location, so we'll

00:02:43.653 --> 00:02:45.131
place them in an HBox.

00:02:45.131 --> 00:02:48.222
An HBox arranges its children horizontally, in

00:02:48.222 --> 00:02:50.104
the order they are supplied.

00:03:01.525 --> 00:03:04.396
The first constructor argument, eight, is the

00:03:04.396 --> 00:03:06.310
gap between adjacent children.

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:09.118
The remaining arguments name the two labels.

00:03:09.118 --> 00:03:11.989
Space inside a label is not what creates this

00:03:11.989 --> 00:03:14.222
gap; the HBox owns the arrangement.

00:03:14.222 --> 00:03:17.157
Soon this whole row will become one child of a

00:03:17.157 --> 00:03:18.050
larger column.

00:03:18.079 --> 00:03:20.990
The instruction is longer than the room value.

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:23.902
We'll give it a separate Label and enable text

00:03:23.902 --> 00:03:24.472
wrapping.

00:03:24.472 --> 00:03:27.510
Wrapping allows a label to use another line when

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:30.484
its assigned width cannot hold all the words on

00:03:30.484 --> 00:03:31.054
one line.

00:03:45.562 --> 00:03:47.691
The true setting permits wrapping.

00:03:47.691 --> 00:03:50.571
It does not promise that every possible window

00:03:50.571 --> 00:03:52.825
size will display the whole message.

00:03:52.825 --> 00:03:55.580
The container must allocate enough width and

00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:58.273
height, so we will inspect the actual label

00:03:58.273 --> 00:03:59.212
after resizing.

00:03:59.245 --> 00:04:02.095
Now build a VBox containing the heading, the

00:04:02.095 --> 00:04:04.426
HBox row, and the instruction label.

00:04:04.426 --> 00:04:07.017
A VBox arranges its children vertically.

00:04:07.017 --> 00:04:09.737
We'll separate adjacent children by twelve

00:04:09.737 --> 00:04:12.845
pixels, then add twenty pixels of padding inside

00:04:12.845 --> 00:04:14.270
the container's edges.

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:26.406
The VBox has three direct children.

00:04:26.406 --> 00:04:29.363
The two location labels are children of the HBox

00:04:29.363 --> 00:04:29.979
inside it.

00:04:29.979 --> 00:04:32.751
Spacing controls the gaps between neighboring

00:04:32.751 --> 00:04:33.306
children.

00:04:33.306 --> 00:04:36.263
Padding controls the space between the container

00:04:36.263 --> 00:04:37.803
boundary and its content.

00:04:37.803 --> 00:04:40.760
Insets describes that edge space; its one-number

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:43.655
constructor applies the same amount to all four

00:04:43.655 --> 00:04:44.025
sides.

00:04:44.062 --> 00:04:46.885
Attach this root to a Scene and give the Stage

00:04:46.885 --> 00:04:48.848
its separate title, Desk Notice.

00:04:48.848 --> 00:04:51.732
The scene requests a four hundred twenty by two

00:04:51.732 --> 00:04:53.389
hundred forty content area.

00:04:53.389 --> 00:04:56.151
After showing the Stage, we'll print the room

00:04:56.151 --> 00:04:58.912
label's text and whether wrapping is enabled.

00:05:19.382 --> 00:05:22.336
We'll finish the supplied start method and main

00:05:22.336 --> 00:05:23.091
entry point.

00:05:23.091 --> 00:05:25.731
The launch call belongs to this standalone

00:05:25.731 --> 00:05:26.171
runner.

00:05:26.171 --> 00:05:28.999
In the notebook, the supplied Fx support uses

00:05:28.999 --> 00:05:32.016
the existing kernel process instead, so you will

00:05:32.016 --> 00:05:34.782
keep the notebook's complete example format.

00:05:42.849 --> 00:05:43.862
Let's click Run.

00:05:43.862 --> 00:05:46.901
We have configured Library as the room value and

00:05:46.901 --> 00:05:47.978
enabled wrapping.

00:05:47.978 --> 00:05:50.954
The console will report those properties, while

00:05:50.954 --> 00:05:53.676
the Desktop will show the heading, row, and

00:05:53.676 --> 00:05:56.399
instruction arranged by the two containers.

00:06:05.304 --> 00:06:08.285
The Reading Desk heading appears above the room

00:06:08.285 --> 00:06:09.553
row and instruction.

00:06:09.553 --> 00:06:12.344
Its larger, bold text gives it a clear role.

00:06:12.344 --> 00:06:14.436
The row reads from left to right.

00:06:14.436 --> 00:06:17.354
The VBox places the three sections from top to

00:06:17.354 --> 00:06:19.954
bottom, with edge space around the group.

00:06:20.029 --> 00:06:23.017
Let's make the native window narrower using its

00:06:23.017 --> 00:06:23.971
resize control.

00:06:23.971 --> 00:06:26.831
The source and label text will stay the same.

00:06:26.831 --> 00:06:29.502
Watch how less available width changes the

00:06:29.502 --> 00:06:32.362
instruction's line breaks, and check that its

00:06:32.362 --> 00:06:34.079
final words remain visible.

00:06:38.795 --> 00:06:41.593
The window is narrower, and the full instruction

00:06:41.593 --> 00:06:43.399
remains available in the label.

00:06:43.399 --> 00:06:45.905
The containers have laid out their children

00:06:45.905 --> 00:06:47.421
again using the new space.

00:06:47.421 --> 00:06:50.102
Wrapping can change where a line ends; it does

00:06:50.102 --> 00:06:52.608
not require the number of lines to increase

00:06:52.608 --> 00:06:54.298
every time the width changes.

00:06:54.298 --> 00:06:57.037
Resizing also does not print a new construction

00:06:57.037 --> 00:06:57.445
report.

00:06:57.516 --> 00:07:00.422
We'll close this notice before changing its

00:07:00.422 --> 00:07:00.895
inputs.

00:07:00.895 --> 00:07:03.869
Closing the last window lets this standalone

00:07:03.869 --> 00:07:05.221
Java process finish.

00:07:05.221 --> 00:07:08.127
A complete run will start a new process and

00:07:08.127 --> 00:07:11.303
construct a new notice at its requested initial

00:07:11.303 --> 00:07:11.641
size.

00:07:15.123 --> 00:07:17.835
For the study room version, use Study Room as

00:07:17.835 --> 00:07:20.186
the heading, Lab A as the room, and the

00:07:20.186 --> 00:07:22.777
instruction about bringing course notes and

00:07:22.777 --> 00:07:24.224
leaving the table ready.

00:07:24.224 --> 00:07:27.056
Set the window title to Room Notice and request

00:07:27.056 --> 00:07:29.588
a four hundred forty by two hundred twenty

00:07:29.588 --> 00:07:29.949
scene.

00:07:29.949 --> 00:07:32.661
Keep the same row, column, gaps, padding, and

00:07:32.661 --> 00:07:33.023
style.

00:08:07.564 --> 00:08:10.374
Before we run, predict the two console lines by

00:08:10.374 --> 00:08:11.868
tracing the getter calls.

00:08:11.868 --> 00:08:14.559
Then predict which values stay unchanged when

00:08:14.559 --> 00:08:16.651
the native window becomes narrower.

00:08:16.651 --> 00:08:19.042
Will the source text change, or will the

00:08:19.042 --> 00:08:21.792
container assign a different width to the same

00:08:21.792 --> 00:08:22.151
label?

00:08:22.151 --> 00:08:24.064
Pause and write your prediction.

00:08:32.174 --> 00:08:35.178
Let's click Run and compare the report with your

00:08:35.178 --> 00:08:35.866
prediction.

00:08:35.866 --> 00:08:38.808
Then we'll inspect the study room notice before

00:08:38.808 --> 00:08:41.499
and after the same native narrowing action.

00:08:49.728 --> 00:08:52.410
The report gives Lab A and wrapping true.

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:55.353
Room Notice is the native window title, while

00:08:55.353 --> 00:08:58.166
Study Room is the heading inside the scene.

00:08:58.166 --> 00:09:01.109
The full instruction is visible, and the HBox

00:09:01.109 --> 00:09:04.118
remains one child between the heading and note

00:09:04.118 --> 00:09:04.903
in the VBox.

00:09:04.976 --> 00:09:07.872
Now narrow this window without changing the

00:09:07.872 --> 00:09:08.410
program.

00:09:08.410 --> 00:09:11.575
Watch the final words of the instruction as the

00:09:11.575 --> 00:09:13.326
available width decreases.

00:09:17.933 --> 00:09:20.559
The instruction still ends with for the next

00:09:20.559 --> 00:09:20.917
group.

00:09:20.917 --> 00:09:22.887
Its line break has moved earlier.

00:09:22.887 --> 00:09:25.751
Both of these tested views use two lines, so the

00:09:25.751 --> 00:09:28.557
visible change is the break point, not the line

00:09:28.557 --> 00:09:28.915
count.

00:09:28.915 --> 00:09:31.660
The padding still belongs to the root's edges,

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:34.346
and the row and column still arrange the same

00:09:34.346 --> 00:09:34.883
children.

00:09:34.962 --> 00:09:37.771
Let's close the window and run the unchanged

00:09:37.771 --> 00:09:38.920
program once more.

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:41.985
That should create a fresh notice at the scene's

00:09:41.985 --> 00:09:44.858
original requested size, rather than preserve

00:09:44.858 --> 00:09:47.412
the width we chose by dragging its edge.

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:52.698
The source is unchanged.

00:09:52.698 --> 00:09:55.387
Click Run again to check that complete

00:09:55.387 --> 00:09:58.430
recreation restores the initial Room Notice

00:09:58.430 --> 00:09:58.925
layout.

00:10:07.037 --> 00:10:10.085
The notice is back at its initial size with the

00:10:10.085 --> 00:10:13.133
same title, heading, location, and instruction.

00:10:13.133 --> 00:10:15.467
That separates the program's initial

00:10:15.467 --> 00:10:18.126
configuration from a user's later resize.

00:10:18.126 --> 00:10:21.239
We'll close this final window after checking the

00:10:21.239 --> 00:10:21.887
full text.

00:10:25.523 --> 00:10:28.107
You built a row inside a column, distinguished

00:10:28.107 --> 00:10:30.634
gaps from edge padding, styled a heading, and

00:10:30.634 --> 00:10:32.713
tested wrapped text in a real window.

00:10:32.713 --> 00:10:35.128
For a campus equipment notice with a longer

00:10:35.128 --> 00:10:37.655
instruction, which parts of this layout would

00:10:37.655 --> 00:10:38.386
you preserve?

00:10:38.386 --> 00:10:41.082
What would you inspect at a smaller width before

00:10:41.082 --> 00:10:42.823
calling the interface readable?
